    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d771">

                <pb facs="0624" n="616" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg616"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/6421</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President of the Export-Import Bank</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Pierson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Chief of the Division of Far Eastern Affairs</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Hornbeck</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-02">August 2, 1937</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In my conference this morning with Secretary Hull it was decided that I should
                    tell Dr. Wang that the Export-Import Bank is favorably <pb facs="0625" n="617"
                        xml:id="pg617"/> disposed to the granting of commercial credits along the
                    lines suggested in the Ambassador’s letter<anchor n="93" xml:id="d771-f1"/> but
                    that we hesitate at this time to make a formal commitment which might soon prove
                    meaningless. Furthermore, that we might undertake to examine an initial
                    transaction with the idea of going through with it if conditions begin to look
                    better.</p>
                <p>In the meantime the Secretary suggests—wisely, I think—that we send a cable to
                    Bingham in London inquiring regarding the alleged British arrangement.<anchor
                        n="94" xml:id="d771-f2"/></p>
                <p>I have drafted a very brief cable which is attached.<anchor n="93"
                        xml:id="d771-f3"/> Will you be good enough to see that this goes forward
                    either in its present form or as you may care to modify it.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Warren Lee
                    Pierson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
